[standards/technology/nodejs/STD-TEC-NODE-001-security.md#31-http-security-headers-helmet]

### 3.1 HTTP Security Headers (helmet)

All Express and Fastify applications **must** apply the `helmet` package to set secure HTTP response headers. Helmet must be registered as the **first** middleware in the chain, before any route or body-parser middleware.

```typescript
import express from 'express';
import helmet from 'helmet';

const app = express();

app.use(
  helmet({
    contentSecurityPolicy: {
      directives: {
        defaultSrc: ["'none'"],
        scriptSrc: ["'self'"],
        connectSrc: ["'self'"],
        imgSrc: ["'self'"],
        frameSrc: ["'none'"],
      },
    },
    hsts: {
      maxAge: 31536000,
      includeSubDomains: true,
      preload: true,
    },
    referrerPolicy: { policy: 'no-referrer' },
    noSniff: true,
  })
);
```

The `X-Powered-By` header **must** be disabled. Express removes it automatically when `helmet` is applied. Fastify disables it via `{ logger: false }` combined with the `@fastify/helmet` plugin. Verify the header is absent in integration tests.

**OWASP A05 (Security Misconfiguration)** — missing or misconfigured HTTP security headers are a common security misconfiguration finding.

