[standards/technology/nodejs/STD-TEC-NODE-001-security.md#32-cors-policy]

### 3.2 CORS Policy

Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) **must** be configured with an explicit allow-list. Wildcard origins (`*`) are prohibited for APIs that process authenticated requests or healthcare data.

```typescript
import cors from 'cors';

const ALLOWED_ORIGINS: string[] = process.env.CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS
  ? process.env.CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS.split(',').map((o) => o.trim())
  : [];

app.use(
  cors({
    origin: (origin, callback) => {
      if (!origin || ALLOWED_ORIGINS.includes(origin)) {
        callback(null, true);
      } else {
        callback(new Error(`CORS: origin '${origin}' not permitted`));
      }
    },
    methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'PATCH', 'DELETE'],
    allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization', 'X-Correlation-ID'],
    credentials: true,
    maxAge: 600,
  })
);
```

- `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` must be set via environment variable — never hardcoded in source.
- BFF services facing browser clients must configure `credentials: true` and set `SameSite` cookie attributes per STD-TEC-NODE guidance on CSRF protection.

